// aeon-os (child_crash): a trivial standalone process that deliberately
// fails with an ORDINARY runtime error (not panic()) - meant to be
// exec()'d from boot.bl0 to prove journalExec() catches and logs a failed
// launch instead of letting it propagate. This is NOT the same claim
// boot.bl0's own final integrity check demonstrates: since exec() now
// runs a loaded program in the SAME Bl0jv2_jVM instance as whatever
// called it (see Bl0jv2_jVM's own exec() doc), a REAL panic() here would
// set the one shared 'panicked' flag and take the WHOLE kernel down with
// it, not just this one process - deliberately using an ordinary error
// instead is what keeps this demo (and the rest of boot.bl0 after it)
// running.

println '  [child_crash] about to fail...';
x = 1 / 0;
println '  [child_crash] unreachable';
